{{ 'dev-article.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
<script src="{{ 'dev-article.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>

{%- comment -%}
  Section: "Article" (dev name `dev-article`). The blog post page — the READING band: breadcrumb,
  title, deck, byline, hero, table of contents and the body. Everything after the body (author card,
  newsletter, related posts, closing panel) is its own section in templates/article.json, so the
  owner can reorder or drop those in the theme editor without touching this one.

  NO FIGMA ARTBOARD EXISTS FOR THIS PAGE — read that before "correcting" any value here.
  Every other dev-* section in this theme is measured off a 1440/375 artboard and QA'd with a
  pixel diff. This one cannot be, because the design does not cover it. The client's 2026-09-16
  mockups (docs/sections/dev-article-blog-structure.md) fix the STRUCTURE and the component list;
  they are concept renders and are never measured. So every value below is borrowed from a section
  that WAS measured, rather than invented:

    * Tiempos Headline Medium 48/1.2 heading  -> dev-hero's --font-heading-size
    * Public Sans Medium 18/1.4 body          -> dev-journal-cards' card copy (Body/Lg)
    * 16px meta, 14px badge / breadcrumb      -> dev-journal-cards' __meta / __badge
    * 32px media radius, 16px inline radius   -> dev-journal-cards' --radius-media / --radius-card
    * 64px / 16px gutters, 80px top spacer    -> the shared section rhythm
    * the back arrow                          -> the SAME exported vector as journal-cards' CTA
    * deck 20px                               -> dev-journal-cards' --card-heading-size step
    * panels (TOC, tip, icon list): cream #FCF5E3 panel, #FFFCF4 tile, 24px / 16px padding
                                              -> dev-pdp-guarantees' panel-and-tile pair
    * panel title 22 / 20px                   -> dev-pdp-guarantees' heading, dev-faq's question
    * chevron + 300ms cubic-bezier(.32,.72,0,1) accordion
                                              -> dev-faq, verbatim (JS duplicated on purpose)
    * quote 28 / 22px                         -> dev-proudest-moments' mobile heading step / --font-h3-size

  Genuinely new, flagged as judgement calls rather than measurements:
    * the READING COLUMN (--measure, 720px): ~72 characters per line at 18px, the middle of the
      45–75 range typography treats as readable. No section in the design has long-form prose.
    * ONE contents dropdown at every width (owner, 2026-10-01: "make sure mobile and desktop match").
      The 2026-09 build moved it into a sticky side rail from 1280px; that rail is gone.
    * the avatar (48px): 2 × --icon-size. Nothing in the design draws a face.
    * the header is LEFT-aligned now (it was centred). The mockup aligns it with the prose, and a
      centred title over a left-aligned column reads as two different pages glued together.

  Content comes from four places, each with a defined empty state (see docs/sections/dev-article.md):
    article.title / .content / .image / .tags / .published_at     Shopify's own fields
    custom.subtitle, custom.hero_caption                               one-line text metafields
    custom.show_contents (empty = on), custom.number_headings (empty = off)     booleans
    custom.author_profile -> `author` metaobject (name, avatar)     falls back to custom.author text
  A post with nothing but a title, a body and a featured image must still render correctly.

  Reading time is NOT computed here. It comes from snippets/dev-article-read-time.liquid, shared
  with dev-journal-cards, so the "4 min read" on the card and on the post it links to are the same
  number by construction. The byline comes from snippets/dev-article-author.liquid, same reason.

  The body goes through snippets/dev-article-body.liquid, which expands the editor's shortcodes
  ([tip], [quote], [icon:…], [caption], [plain]) and gives every <h2> an anchor. The table of
  contents is then read back out of THAT output, so a TOC link can never point at a heading id
  the body does not carry.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%} Fixed design tokens — borrowed from the measured sections, see above. Shared with the
   component library page, hence a snippet. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- render 'dev-article-tokens', scope: '.dev-article' -%}

{%- liquid
  assign article_tag = article.tags | first
  assign blog_url = blog.url | default: routes.root_url

  comment
    Breadcrumb (owner, 2026-09-28): News and Guest Review become Snooze and Humble Home.
      * First crumb: the blog, labelled by the section setting. The blog is called News in the
        admin but is branded Larke Snooze on the index. Blank falls back to the blog's own title.
      * Second crumb: the post's custom.breadcrumb_tag when set, a tag the editor picks to name
        the post's series (Humble Home), otherwise its first tag as before. It is a TAG, not free
        text, so it always names a real series of posts.
    The first tag still drives the card badge and articleSection; only the crumb changes.
  endcomment
  assign crumb_blog = section.settings.blog_crumb_label | default: blog.title
  comment
    2026-10-01: the blog index is retired (it redirects to the blog list on Our Story), so the first
    crumb links to the section setting's URL — /pages/our-story#journal — and the second crumb is
    plain text: its tagged page would only redirect to the same list.
  endcomment
  assign crumb_blog_url = section.settings.blog_crumb_link | default: blog_url
  assign crumb_tag = article.metafields.custom.breadcrumb_tag.value | default: article_tag | strip

  assign deck = article.metafields.custom.subtitle.value
  assign hero_caption = article.metafields.custom.hero_caption.value
  assign author_profile = article.metafields.custom.author_profile.value

  comment
    Show contents: EMPTY MEANS ON — only an explicit `false` hides the dropdown (an unset metafield's
    value is nil, and nil is not false).
    Number headings: EMPTY MEANS OFF since 2026-10-01 — Ruben asked on the 2026-10-01 call for the
    "1. 2. 3." before body headings to go, on desktop and mobile. The field stays as an opt-in:
    only an explicit `true` numbers a post's headings.
  endcomment
  assign toc_enabled = true
  if article.metafields.custom.show_contents.value == false
    assign toc_enabled = false
  endif
  assign numbered = false
  if article.metafields.custom.number_headings.value == true
    assign numbered = true
  endif

  # Number contents (owner, 2026-10-05: "option to have numbers in In this Snooze"). Its own
  # opt-in, separate from Number headings — empty means off, like Number headings.
  assign toc_numbered = false
  if article.metafields.custom.number_contents.value == true
    assign toc_numbered = true
  endif

  assign avatar = author_profile.avatar.value
-%}

{%- capture article_body -%}{%- render 'dev-article-body', html: article.content -%}{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  The table of contents, read back out of the processed body. Every h2 heading there opens with
  its id attribute FIRST (the body snippet writes it that way precisely so this split can rely on
  it). No angle brackets in this comment: theme-check 4.x parses them as real tags even inside a
  comment and then reports false syntax errors on the markup below.

  The contents list is unnumbered (owner, 2026-09-28) and body headings are unnumbered by default
  (2026-10-01). A post that opts in via Number headings gets them, and a `[plain]` heading
  (class="larke-plain") stays out of that count.

  Fewer than three headings and there is nothing to navigate: a two-item contents list is noise
  above a short post, so it is simply not drawn.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign toc_items = ''
  assign toc_count = 0
  assign h2_open = '<h2 id="'
  if toc_enabled and article_body contains h2_open
    assign heads = article_body | split: h2_open
    for chunk in heads
      if forloop.first
        continue
      endif
      assign slug = chunk | split: '"' | first
      assign head = chunk | split: '</h2>' | first
      assign attrs = head | split: '>' | first
      assign label = head | remove_first: attrs | remove_first: '>' | strip_html | strip
      assign item_class = 'dev-article__toc-item'
      if attrs contains 'larke-plain'
        assign item_class = item_class | append: ' dev-article__toc-item--plain'
      endif
      capture item
        echo '<li class="' | append: item_class | append: '"><a class="dev-article__toc-link" href="#' | append: slug | append: '" data-toc-link>' | append: label | append: '</a></li>'
      endcapture
      assign toc_items = toc_items | append: item
      assign toc_count = toc_count | plus: 1
    endfor
  endif
  assign show_toc = false
  if toc_count >= 3
    assign show_toc = true
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  The contents dropdown, dropped into the body just before the first heading, i.e. after the
  intro, where the mockup puts it. The same dropdown at every width.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if show_toc -%}
  {%- capture toc_inline -%}
    <details class="dev-article__toc" data-toc>
      <summary class="dev-article__toc-summary">
        <span class="dev-article__toc-title">{{ section.settings.toc_label | escape }}</span>
        <svg class="dev-article__toc-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M19 9L12 16L5 9" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
        </svg>
      </summary>
      <ol class="dev-article__toc-list{% if toc_numbered %} dev-article__toc-list--numbered{% endif %}" role="list">{{ toc_items }}</ol>
    </details>
  {%- endcapture -%}
  {%- assign toc_marker = toc_inline | append: h2_open -%}
  {%- assign article_body = article_body | replace_first: h2_open, toc_marker -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  STRUCTURED DATA — BlogPosting + BreadcrumbList, written here rather than with Shopify's
  `article | structured_data`. Checked on the store preview 2026-09-22, the built-in one:
    * credited the STAFF ACCOUNT ("Misha Gavura") while the page says "By The Humble Home" — Google
      reads the author for E-E-A-T, and a byline that disagrees with its markup is a trust signal
      pointing the wrong way;
    * put the raw body in articleBody, shortcodes and all ("[tip] Top tip …");
    * had no publisher logo, section, keywords, word count or language.
  The author follows the page byline exactly (Author profile -> custom.author -> staff account),
  and every text field comes from snippets/dev-article-plain-text.liquid, the same cleaned text
  the meta description uses.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign ld_url = canonical_url | default: article.url
  capture ld_description
    render 'dev-article-plain-text', html: article.content, words: 40
  endcapture
  if deck != blank
    assign ld_description = deck
  endif
  if article.excerpt != blank
    assign ld_description = article.excerpt | strip_html | strip
  endif
  capture ld_text
    render 'dev-article-plain-text', html: article.content
  endcapture
  assign ld_words = ld_text | split: ' ' | size

  assign ld_author_name = author_profile.name.value
  if ld_author_name == blank
    capture ld_author_name
      render 'dev-article-author', article: article
    endcapture
    assign ld_author_name = ld_author_name | strip
  endif
  assign ld_author_url = author_profile.link.value
  assign ld_author_role = author_profile.role.value

  assign ld_logo = settings.logo | default: shop.brand.logo
-%}
<script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BlogPosting",
    "@id": {{ ld_url | append: '#article' | json }},
    "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": {{ ld_url | json }} },
    "url": {{ ld_url | json }},
    "headline": {{ article.title | strip_html | truncate: 110, '' | json }},
    "description": {{ ld_description | json }},
    {%- if article.image -%}
      "image": [
        {
          "@type": "ImageObject",
          "url": {{ article.image | image_url: width: 1920 | prepend: 'https:' | json }},
          "width": {{ article.image.width | at_most: 1920 | json }},
          "height": {{ article.image.height | times: 1920 | divided_by: article.image.width | at_most: article.image.height | json }}
        }
      ],
    {%- endif -%}
    "datePublished": {{ article.published_at | date: '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%:z' | json }},
    "dateModified": {{ article.updated_at | date: '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%:z' | json }},
    {%- if ld_author_name != blank -%}
      "author": [
        {
          "@type": "Person",
          "name": {{ ld_author_name | json }}
          {%- if ld_author_url != blank -%}, "url": {{ ld_author_url | json }}{%- endif -%}
          {%- if ld_author_role != blank -%}, "jobTitle": {{ ld_author_role | json }}{%- endif -%}
          {%- if avatar != blank -%}, "image": {{ avatar | image_url: width: 256 | prepend: 'https:' | json }}{%- endif -%}
        }
      ],
    {%- endif -%}
    "publisher": {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "name": {{ shop.name | json }},
      "url": {{ request.origin | append: routes.root_url | json }}
      {%- if ld_logo != blank -%},
        "logo": { "@type": "ImageObject", "url": {{ ld_logo | image_url: width: 600 | prepend: 'https:' | json }} }
      {%- endif -%}
    },
    "isPartOf": { "@type": "Blog", "@id": {{ request.origin | append: blog.url | json }}, "name": {{ blog.title | json }} },
    {%- if article_tag != blank -%}
      "articleSection": {{ article_tag | json }},
      "keywords": {{ article.tags | join: ', ' | json }},
    {%- endif -%}
    "wordCount": {{ ld_words | json }},
    "inLanguage": {{ request.locale.iso_code | json }}
  }
</script>
<script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    "itemListElement": [
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": {{ crumb_blog | json }}, "item": {{ request.origin | append: crumb_blog_url | json }} },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": {{ article.title | json }} }
    ]
  }
</script>

<div
  class="dev-article{% if numbered %} dev-article--numbered{% endif %}"
  style="
    --bg: {{ section.settings.background_color }};
    --color: {{ section.settings.text_color }};
    --muted: {{ section.settings.meta_color }};
    --badge-bg: {{ section.settings.badge_color }};
    --rule: {{ section.settings.rule_color }};
    --panel-bg: {{ section.settings.panel_color }};
    --tile-bg: {{ section.settings.tile_color }};
    --accent: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
    --pt: {{ section.settings.padding_top }}px;
    --pb: {{ section.settings.padding_bottom }}px;
  "
  data-article
>
  <article class="dev-article__article">
  <div class="dev-article__inner">
    {%- if section.settings.back_label != blank -%}
      <a class="dev-article__back dev-btn dev-btn--text dev-btn--icon-left" href="{{ blog_url }}">
        {%- comment -%} Same vector as dev-journal-cards' CTA, mirrored to point back. {%- endcomment -%}
        <svg class="dev-article__back-icon dev-btn__icon" width="24" height="24" viewBox="99 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M106 16L102 12L106 8M102 12L120 12" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
        </svg>
        <span class="dev-btn__label">{{ section.settings.back_label | escape }}</span>
      </a>
    {%- endif -%}

    <header class="dev-article__head">
      {%- if section.settings.show_breadcrumb -%}
        <nav class="dev-article__crumbs" aria-label="{{ section.settings.breadcrumb_label | escape }}">
          <ol class="dev-article__crumbs-list" role="list">
            <li class="dev-article__crumb"><a class="dev-article__crumb-link" href="{{ crumb_blog_url }}">{{ crumb_blog | escape }}</a></li>
            {%- if crumb_tag != blank -%}
              <li class="dev-article__crumb"><span class="dev-article__crumb-text">{{ crumb_tag | escape }}</span></li>
            {%- endif -%}
          </ol>
        </nav>
      {%- endif -%}

      <h1 class="dev-article__title">{{ article.title | escape }}</h1>

      {%- if deck != blank -%}
        <p class="dev-article__deck">{{ deck | escape }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        The byline row: avatar (only when the author metaobject has one) + "By <name>". The name
        prefers the metaobject, then the existing custom.author / article.author chain, so posts
        written before the metaobject existed keep exactly the byline they had.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign byline = author_profile.name.value
        if byline == blank
          capture byline
            render 'dev-article-author', article: article
          endcapture
        endif
      -%}
      {%- if byline != blank -%}
        <div class="dev-article__byline">
          {%- if avatar != blank -%}
            {{
              avatar
              | image_url: width: 96, height: 96, crop: 'center'
              | image_tag: loading: 'eager', width: 48, height: 48, alt: '', class: 'dev-article__avatar'
            }}
          {%- endif -%}
          <p class="dev-article__author">
            {%- if section.settings.author_prefix != blank -%}{{ section.settings.author_prefix | escape }} {% endif -%}
            {{- byline | strip | escape -}}
          </p>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        The meta line is built from the parts that actually exist — no dangling separators.
        The dot is its own element (owner, 2026-09-28: "make the separator dot darker for better
        contrast"): the words stay in the muted meta colour, the dot takes the full text colour.
        It is aria-hidden — a screen reader hears "5 min read, September 22, 2026", not "dot".
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- capture read_minutes -%}{%- render 'dev-article-read-time', article: article -%}{%- endcapture -%}
      <p class="dev-article__meta">
        <span>{{ read_minutes | strip }} {{ section.settings.read_time_label | escape }}</span>
        {%- if section.settings.show_date -%}
          <span class="dev-article__meta-sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span class="dev-article__visually-hidden">, </span>
          <time datetime="{{ article.published_at | date: '%Y-%m-%d' }}">{{ article.published_at | date: format: 'date' }}</time>
        {%- endif -%}
      </p>
    </header>

    {%- if article.image != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The alt is resolved in its own assign, NOT inline in the image_tag arguments. Liquid does
        not allow a filter pipe inside an argument list: `alt: article.image.alt | default: x` is
        parsed as "pipe the whole image_tag output into `default`", which swallows every argument
        after it — the `class` never reached image_tag and the <img> rendered unclassed, so its
        `width: 100%` never applied. This bit once; keep the assign.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        THE HERO IS THIS PAGE'S LCP ELEMENT — confirmed with a PerformanceObserver on the rendered
        page at both widths, not assumed. Everything below follows from that.

        `sizes` said 1312px and the box has never been wider than 960. 1312 is the Figma content
        frame (1440 − 2×64), which is the width of the SECTION; the image lives inside
        .dev-article__inner, capped at --media-w: 960px. The 37% overshoot was enough to push the
        browser off the 1000w entry and all the way onto 2600w:

          box 960 CSS @ DPR2  ->  1920 device px needed
          shipped             ->  2600w, 519,042 B

        On the CDN, same image: 2600 = 519,042 B, 1920 = 292,806 B, 1920 at q75 = 151,846 B, and
        1920x1280 cropped at q75 = 69,078 B. The crop is the bigger half — see --ratio-media.

        The ladder now stops at 1920 for the same reason — 960 × 2 is the most a correct `sizes`
        can ever ask for, and the entries above it were unreachable weight in the srcset. Below
        1088 the cap stops binding and the padding takes over, hence the middle branch: 64px each
        side above 768, 16px below.

        `quality` cannot be passed blind — image_url raises "quality can't be used with ext png" and
        the src renders as an error string. This is a merchant's featured image, so the extension is
        read off the URL first. A png keeps the correct width and skips the recompression rather
        than being flattened to jpg, which would drop any alpha it does have.

        fetchpriority high: `eager` only removes it from the lazy queue. Being the LCP element, it
        should also outrank the rest of the page's subresources, and nothing else here competes.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign hero_alt = article.image.alt | default: article.title
        assign hero_ext = article.image | image_url: width: 1 | split: '?' | first | split: '.' | last | downcase

        comment
          1920x1920 is --ratio-media (1/1 since 2026-10-05, was 3/2) at the widest the box ever gets:
          960 CSS at DPR 2.
          `image_tag`'s `widths:` scales the height with each rung, so every srcset entry keeps the
          ratio and the browser is handed the box rather than a portrait to trim.
        endcomment
        if hero_ext == 'gif'
          assign hero_src = article.image | image_url: width: 1920, height: 1920, crop: 'center'
        elsif hero_ext == 'png'
          assign hero_src = article.image | image_url: width: 1920, height: 1920, crop: 'center', format: 'webp', quality: 75
        else
          assign hero_src = article.image | image_url: width: 1920, height: 1920, crop: 'center', quality: 75
        endif
      -%}
      <figure class="dev-article__figure">
      <div class="dev-article__media">
        {{
          hero_src
          | image_tag:
            loading: 'eager',
            fetchpriority: 'high',
            widths: '400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 1000, 1120, 1280, 1440, 1600, 1920',
            sizes: '(min-width: 1088px) 960px, (min-width: 769px) calc(100vw - 128px), calc(100vw - 32px)',
            alt: hero_alt,
            class: 'dev-article__image'
        }}
      </div>
      {%- if hero_caption != blank -%}
        <figcaption class="dev-article__caption">{{ hero_caption | escape }}</figcaption>
      {%- endif -%}
      </figure>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  {%- comment -%} The reading band: the body at the 720px measure, centred under the hero. {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="dev-article__layout">
    {%- comment -%}
      article.content is merchant HTML from the Shopify editor — already safe, never escaped. It
      arrives here already processed by snippets/dev-article-body.liquid (shortcodes, lazy images,
      heading anchors). The class is `dev-article__body`, deliberately NOT Horizon's `.rte`, which
      is a stock class and would drag stock rules in with it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="dev-article__body" data-article-body>
      {{ article_body }}
    </div>
  </div>

  {%- if section.settings.back_label != blank -%}
    <div class="dev-article__foot">
      <a class="dev-article__back dev-btn dev-btn--text dev-btn--icon-left" href="{{ blog_url }}">
        <svg class="dev-article__back-icon dev-btn__icon" width="24" height="24" viewBox="99 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M106 16L102 12L106 8M102 12L120 12" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
        </svg>
        <span class="dev-btn__label">{{ section.settings.back_label | escape }}</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
  </article>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Article",
  "tag": "section",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "header", "content": "Content" },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Per-article fields (subtitle, hero caption, author card, contents on/off) live on the article itself, under Metafields. Components inside the body are typed as shortcodes — see the Article components page."
    },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_breadcrumb", "label": "Show breadcrumb", "default": true },
    { "type": "text", "id": "breadcrumb_label", "label": "Breadcrumb label (screen readers)", "default": "Breadcrumb" },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "blog_crumb_label",
      "label": "Blog name in breadcrumb",
      "info": "Leave empty to use the blog's own title. The second level is the post's 'Breadcrumb tag' field, or its first tag.",
      "default": "Snooze"
    },
    {
      "type": "url",
      "id": "blog_crumb_link",
      "label": "Breadcrumb link",
      "info": "Where the first breadcrumb goes. Empty = the blog. Larke: /pages/our-story#journal"
    },
    { "type": "text", "id": "toc_label", "label": "Contents heading", "default": "In this article" },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "back_label",
      "label": "Back link label",
      "info": "Leave empty to hide the back links. The breadcrumb and the related-articles section already lead back to the blog.",
      "default": "Back to Journal"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "read_time_label",
      "label": "Reading time label",
      "info": "Set the article's 'Reading time' metafield to state the minutes. Left empty, it is estimated from the word count.",
      "default": "min read"
